Actually, D&D is the greatest problem, and the thing which will make
Ray's approach completely impossible. NetHack, and a whole host of other
games, "borrowed" the Law/Neu/Cha-Good/Neu/Evil set of values from D&D,
together with hitdice, ideas on spellcasting, and so on. I don't know
how much of that is copyrightable, but I strongly suspect enough of it
is to make NetHack without them an unrecognisably different game.

> Tolkien's works may be under copyright, but there is such a thing as
> "fair use".

There is, but continuous use in a computer game does not fall under it.
And no, being given away for free does _not_ make it fair.

> Or did you want to get rid of all the gnomes, dwarves, elves, orcs and
> (tricksy) hobbits(es) while you're at it?

The first three are from the general background mythology of Europe, so
they won't be a problem at all - fantasy authors stole them from the
same source as NetHack did. (In fact, NH elves are closer to the
original than Tolkien's elves.) Orcs were partly taken from mythology
(Orcus is from the same etymological background - /Lat./, a devouring
monster), but strongly modified by Tolkien.
The stinker here is the hobbit. He was entirely invented by Tolkien,
although of course influenced on the other kinds of Small Folk. Strictly
speaking, he - and Elbereth, and so on - should not be in NetHack
without a license, nor should material from other authors such as Sir*
Terence Pratchett or Michael Moorcock.

The thing is, references to Blind Io or Orcrist can easily be ripped
out, or replaced by something similar-but-not-too-similar. Rerefences to
STR/CON/DEX/INT/WIS/CHA... that's going to be a bit harder. And unless
I'm very much mistaken, the game that first introduced the idea of
having (and increasing) those separate stats still is very much under
copyright.

